# Analytical spring motion, wired to the clocks you already use.

`tensum` is an analytical spring solver with a GSAP plugin, a direct controller, and deterministic output helpers. The spring state comes from absolute time. Seeking, reversing, or sampling at another frame rate does not change the path.

The GSAP effect is the usual starting point. It calculates the physical settling duration before GSAP places the tween on a timeline, so later children start where the spring actually ends.

## Install

Install Spring beside GSAP. GSAP is a peer dependency, which keeps the application and the plugin on the same timeline instance.

```bash
pnpm add tensum gsap
```

The package is ESM-only. The tested Node.js lines are 20.19.x, 22 from 22.12.0, and 24.x. Browser integration runs in Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit. TypeScript 6 projects should use `node16`, `nodenext`, or `bundler` module resolution.

## Quick start

Register the plugin once, then add a spring through the timeline effect.

```ts
import { gsap } from 'gsap';
import { TensumPlugin, springPresets } from 'tensum';

gsap.registerPlugin(TensumPlugin);

gsap.timeline().spring(card, {
  x: 320,
  from: { x: 0 },
  parameters: springPresets.snappy()
});
```

No GSAP `duration` or `ease` is required. The physical parameters and settling tolerances determine the duration and trajectory.

<Callout title="Choose the API by clock ownership">
Use <Code>timeline.spring()</Code> when GSAP owns composition. Use <Code>springTo()</Code> when you need direct controls such as <Code>pause()</Code>, <Code>seek()</Code>, or <Code>playbackReverse()</Code>. Use <Code>createSpring()</Code> when you only need analytical samples.
</Callout>

## timeline.spring()

<ApiSignature>timeline.spring(targets, vars, position?): Timeline</ApiSignature>

The registered effect preflights every target before adding its tween. Sequential children, staggered targets, and nested timelines receive the final duration immediately.

```ts
const timeline = gsap.timeline();

timeline
  .spring(card, {
    x: 320,
    from: { x: 0 },
    parameters: { mass: 1, stiffness: 180, damping: 24 }
  })
  .spring(card, {
    x: 80,
    from: { x: 320 },
    parameters: { mass: 1, stiffness: 240, damping: 26 }
  });
```

Ordinary GSAP driver options belong under `tween`. Tensum derives and owns `duration` and `ease`.

```ts
timeline.spring(cards, {
  y: 0,
  from: { y: -24 },
  parameters: springPresets.snappy(),
  tween: {
    stagger: 0.06,
    repeat: 1,
    yoyo: true
  }
});
```

Use `createMotionSpringTween(targets, vars)` when another piece of code needs the preflighted tween instead of the extended timeline method.

## Starting state

Preflight reads the target when the effect is created. That is correct when the element already has its starting value. If an earlier timeline child will change the target later, pass the future state through `from`.

```ts
timeline.spring(card, {
  x: 80,
  from: { x: 320 },
  velocity: { x: -240 },
  parameters: springPresets.snappy()
});
```

`velocity` is measured in property units per second. A single number applies to every property; a map can set each property independently.

<Callout title="Why from is explicit" kind="warning">
An effect created before playback cannot inspect a state that an earlier child has not written yet. <Code>from</Code> makes that future snapshot part of the timeline definition, so layout is fully determined during construction.
</Callout>

## springTo()

<ApiSignature>springTo(target, vars): SpringController</ApiSignature>

`springTo()` uses GSAP property access and ticking but returns direct playback controls. Built-in transform properties are `x`, `y`, `scale`, and `rotation`; other numeric properties go in `targets`.

```ts
import { springTo } from 'tensum';

const motion = springTo(card, {
  x: 600,
  rotation: '30deg',
  velocity: { x: 1250 },
  spring: {
    mass: 1,
    stiffness: 180,
    damping: 24,
    settle: { position: 0.1, velocity: 0.1 }
  },
  targets: { opacity: 1 }
});

motion.pause();
motion.seek(0.2);
motion.resume();
```

The controller also exposes `play()`, `stop()`, `playbackReverse()`, `kill()`, `getSnapshot()`, its GSAP `tween`, and the per-property analytical solutions in `springs`.

Starting a newer Tensum track on the same target and property performs a velocity-preserving handoff. The newer track becomes the only writer without restarting from rest.

DOM `x` and `y` values default to pixels, while DOM rotation defaults to degrees. Ordinary object properties stay as unitless numbers unless their current value, destination, adapter, or `units` configuration explicitly supplies a unit.

## Parameters

Every physical spring uses three values.

| Parameter | Constraint | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `mass` | greater than `0` | More mass resists acceleration and changes the response time. |
| `stiffness` | greater than `0` | Higher stiffness pulls toward the target more strongly. |
| `damping` | `0` or greater | Higher damping removes oscillation and velocity sooner. |

You can provide those values directly or derive them from controls that are easier to tune.

```ts
import { springParameters, springPresets } from 'tensum';

const tuned = springParameters.fromPerceptualDuration({
  duration: 0.5,
  bounce: 0.15
});

const smooth = springPresets.smooth();
const snappy = springPresets.snappy({ duration: 0.42 });
const bouncy = springPresets.bouncy({ extraBounce: 0.08 });
```

The converter also accepts response, visual duration, and settling-duration inputs. `springCharacteristics(parameters)` returns the damping ratio, regime, angular frequency, response, bounce, and perceptual duration for an existing physical configuration.

## Settlement

A damped spring approaches equilibrium asymptotically, so the runtime needs a practical completion boundary. A spring is settled when both the remaining distance and velocity stay inside their tolerances for the future tail.

```text
abs(position - target) <= positionEpsilon
abs(velocity) <= velocityEpsilon
```

The public `settle` input uses shorter property names.

| Input | Default role |
| --- | --- |
| `position` | Maximum remaining distance from the target. |
| `velocity` | Maximum remaining speed. |
| `maxDuration` | Search boundary for a finite settlement. |
| `refinementIterations` | Precision of the final duration search. |

`onLogicalComplete` and `onSettle` describe different boundaries. Logical completion follows the requested perceptual timing. Settlement means the physical tolerances were met.

## Unsettled springs

An undamped moving spring never reaches physical rest. The `unsettled` option decides what happens at `maxDuration`.

| Policy | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `stop` | Freeze at the analytical state at `maxDuration`. This is the default. |
| `snap` | Write the target value at `maxDuration`. |
| `continue` | Keep an infinite GSAP driver and continue analytical sampling. |
| `error` | Reject the animation before playback. |

`onUnsettled` runs when a finite boundary is crossed without settlement. `onSettle` does not run for that track.

<Callout title="Continue owns an infinite driver">
Covering a <Code>continue</Code> track with a newer property track changes which track writes the property, but it does not end the old driver. Kill the property or tween when the continuing spring is no longer needed.
</Callout>

## CSS linear()

The CSS subpath exports an adaptive `linear()` approximation of the same analytical trajectory.

```ts
import { createSpring } from 'tensum';
import { springToCSSLinear } from 'tensum/css';

const spring = createSpring({
  from: 0,
  to: 1,
  velocity: 0,
  mass: 1,
  stiffness: 180,
  damping: 24
});

const css = springToCSSLinear(spring, { maxError: 0.001 });

element.style.transition = `transform ${css.duration}s ${css.easing}`;
```

The result contains the easing string, duration, certified maximum approximation error, and the rounded normalized samples used by the string. `maxError` is a hard normalized-progress bound that includes interpolation and serialization rounding. If an explicit duration would require too large a final snap, or `precision`, `maxDepth`, or `maxSamples` cannot satisfy the bound, the function throws a `RangeError` instead of returning a less accurate curve.

## API surface

The package root groups the public API by job.

| Job | Main exports |
| --- | --- |
| GSAP composition | `TensumPlugin`, `createMotionSpringTween` |
| Direct controller | `springTo`, `SUPPORTED_PROPERTIES` |
| Analytical solver | `createSpring`, `createSpringModel`, `createVectorSpring` |
| Parameter conversion | `springParameters`, `springPresets`, `springCharacteristics` |
| Reactive values | `createSpringValue`, `createAdditiveSpringValue` |
| Sampling | `createSpringKeyframes`, `velocityFromSamples` |
| Inertia | `createInertia`, `snapToGrid` |
| CSS | `springToCSSLinear` from `tensum/css` |
| Coupled systems | `createCoupledSpringSystem` from `tensum/coupled` |

`createSpring()` returns `positionAt(time)`, `velocityAt(time)`, `stateAt(time)`, settlement information, and `retarget(target, time)`. It does not schedule frames or write to the DOM.

`createAdditiveSpringValue()` composes independent physical contributions and exposes `settle` and `unsettled`. It has no perceptual `timing` option or `logicalComplete` event because the contributions do not share one logical boundary.

## Lifecycle

GSAP owns timeline playback, seeking, repeats, yoyo cycles, context reverts, and property-level kills. Spring samples the corresponding analytical time and keeps an ownership registry per target and property.

- Killing one property removes only that spring track.
- Killing the final property releases the internal driver track.
- `invalidate()` keeps the effect snapshot captured when it was created.
- Context revert and interruption release active ownership.
- A completed track remains available as a terminal handoff baseline until an external write replaces it.

If application code writes a different value after completion, the next implicit handoff detects the change and starts from the value currently on the target. An explicit `from` always wins.

## Runtime support

The package ships ESM, TypeScript declarations, source maps, and declaration maps. CommonJS `require()` is intentionally unsupported; CommonJS code can use dynamic `import()` while migrating.

Built-in DOM properties accept numbers and single-unit numeric strings such as `24px`, `30deg`, and `100%`. A unit mismatch throws before playback. Custom adapters can provide read and write behavior for values that GSAP cannot address directly.

The [interactive playground](/#playground) is useful for tuning parameters. The [landing page](/) shows the same solver across controller, GSAP timeline, and CSS output.
